  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a6334982dcae08e4698af5d208c769f4, type: 3}
  m_Name: Question2_Wheatfield
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  description:
  - "Van Gogh painted this broad field of wheat beneath a dark, unsettled sky while
    he was in Auvers sur Oise in July 1890. He intended to express both the vitality
    of the countryside and his own poignant inner feelings. He wrote of \u201Csadness,
    extreme loneliness\u201D while also noting what he considered \u201Chealthy and
    fortifying about the countryside.\u201D The painting shows a central red path
    cutting through golden wheat toward a horizon under a storm filled sky, with
    black crows flying above. The path seems unresolved, hinting at movement without
    destination. The crows carry symbolic weight and are often read as signs of mortality
    or transition."
  - In summary, Wheatfield with Crows blends landscape and emotional intensity. It
    offers a scene of rural life under conditions that feel charged and unsettled.
    The technical features, including strong contrasts, textured surfaces, dynamic
    marks, and a dominant sky, amplify the emotional content. Van Gogh uses the wheat
    field and the flight of crows not simply to depict nature but to convey a journey,
    a sense of isolation, and a life in transition.
  awnsers:
